{
  "prompt": "How does the concept of backpropagation expand from simple chain-based models (like MLPs) to handle complex structures represented by a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)?",
  "reference_answer": "All detailed examples of backpropagation were initially shown for a simple chain-based DAG, where components simply go one after the other. To apply backpropagation to any DAG, two key operations must be introduced: merging and branching. These operations allow the gradient to pass through any structure, whether it is a simple chain or a complex graph. When components merge, the incoming changes are aggregated by summing the gradients. This allows the definition of backprop to expand to any DAG structure.",
