{
  "prompt": "Explain the conceptual differences between autoencoding and masked prediction, detailing how their underlying mechanisms lead to different types of learned representations and when each approach might be preferred.",
  "reference_answer": "Autoencoders control compression via a dimensional bottleneck, forcing the network to remove redundancies. In contrast, masked prediction controls compression by requiring mutual information between the inputs and the outputs being predicted. Empirically, masked prediction networks learn a representation that is more semantic and linearly decodes categories better than autoencoders. One hypothesis suggests autoencoders might be learning shortcuts or copying local information, whereas masked prediction is closer to the actual prediction problems we care about.",
